{"cells":[{"source":"<a href=\"https://www.kaggle.com/code/masum1834e/vector-database?scriptVersionId=369049695\" target=\"_blank\"><img align=\"left\" alt=\"Kaggle\" title=\"Open in Kaggle\" src=\"https://kaggle.com/static/images/open-in-kaggle.svg\"></a>","metadata":{},"cell_type":"markdown"},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"1ee28c73","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.012342,"end_time":"2026-11-13T06:31:16.376533+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-13T06:31:16.364191+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["Vector databases are one of the most important technologies in modern AI engineering. They are widely used in RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation), semantic search, recommendation systems, AI agents, image search, document retrieval, and multimodal AI applications.\n","\n","# Traditioanl Database vs Vector Database\n","A traditional database searches for data based on exact values, conditions, and relationships. A vector database searches for data based on meaning and similarity. Both databases store and retrieve information, but they solve different problems.\n","\n","## Traditioanl Storage\n","\n","A traditional database such as PostgreSQL or MySQL stores structured data in tables (rows and columns).\n","\n","We generally search using:\n","\n","- Exact values.\n","- Partial text matches.\n","- Numeric comparisons.\n","- Filtering conditions.\n","- Relationships between tables.\n","- Sorting and aggregation.\n","\n","Suppose you have an e-commerce application containing these products:\n","\n","| ID | Product            |\n","| -- | ------------------ |\n","| 1  | iPhone 17          |\n","| 2  | Samsung Galaxy S25 |\n","| 3  | MacBook Pro        |\n","| 4  | Nike Running Shoes |\n","| 5  | Adidas Sneakers    |\n","\n","**Example**\n","\n","Suppose the user searches:\n","\n","> Find the product named iPhone 17.\n","\n","SQL:\n","\n","```\n","SELECT *\n","FROM products\n","WHERE name = 'iPhone 17';\n","```\n","\n","The database finds the record where the `name` column exactly matches the supplied value.\n","\n","**Limitation**\n","\n","Traditional databases do not inherently understand the semantic meaning of your search query.\n","\n","For example:\n","\n","Suppose your database contains:\n","\n","- Running Shoes\n","- Gaming Mobile\n","- Formal Pant\n","\n","The user searches:\n","\n","> I need shoes for jogging.\n","\n","A traditional SQL query such as:\n","\n","```\n","SELECT *\n","FROM products\n","WHERE name LIKE '%jogging%';\n","```\n","\n","will probably return nothing.\n","\n","Why? Because the word `jogging` doesn't exist in the product names.\n","\n","However, humans understand that jogging shoes, running shoes, sports footwear, and athletic sneakers can describe similar things. This is where vector databases become useful.\n","\n","## Vector Storage\n","\n","A vector database is a specialized database designed to store, index, and search high dimensional numerical vectors efficiently. Traditional database matches text, not necessarily meaning. A vector database solves this problem by searching based on semantic similarity.\n","\n","It understands that:\n","\n","- Smartphone is related to iPhone.\n","- Smartphone is related to Samsung Galaxy.\n","- Laptop is related to MacBook.\n","- Running shoes are related to sneakers.\n","\n","How? By converting text into numerical representations called vector embeddings.\n","\n","# Vector\n","\n","Before understanding vector databases, you need to understand vectors. A vector is simply an **ordered** collection of numbers.\n","\n","For example:\n","\n","$$\n","A = [0.2, 0.5, 0.8]\n","$$\n","\n","This is a 3 dimensional vector.\n","\n","It contains three numerical dimensions:\n","\n","- Dimension 1 = 0.2\n","- Dimension 2 = 0.5\n","- Dimension 3 = 0.8\n","\n","You can think of vectors as points in a mathematical space. In AI, vectors represent information.\n","\n","![Vector](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/masum184e/handbook/main/static/img/ai-engineering/vector.png)"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":1,"id":"93c32cb1","metadata":{"_cell_guid":"b1076dfc-b9ad-4769-8c92-a6c4dae69d19","_uuid":"8f2839f25d086af736a60e9eeb907d3b93b6e0e5","execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-13T06:31:16.399918Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-13T06:31:16.399098Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-13T06:31:54.566681Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-13T06:31:54.565982Z"},"papermill":{"duration":38.181493,"end_time":"2026-11-13T06:31:54.56865+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-13T06:31:16.387157+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"name":"stderr","output_type":"stream","text":["Warning: You are sending unauthenticated requests to the HF Hub. Please set a HF_TOKEN to enable higher rate limits and faster downloads.\n"]},{"data":{"application/vnd.jupyter.widget-view+json":{"model_id":"03625aa97ba94538b20b5a7a503c4e69","version_major":2,"version_minor":0},"text/plain":["modules.json:   0%|          | 0.00/349 [00:00<?, ?B/s]"]},"metadata":{},"output_type":"display_data"},{"data":{"application/vnd.jupyter.widget-view+json":{"model_id":"ea5c7291cae94364a12cb2ed1d005c3b","version_major":2,"version_minor":0},"text/plain":["config_sentence_transformers.json:   0%|          | 0.00/116 [00:00<?, ?B/s]"]},"metadata":{},"output_type":"display_data"},{"data":{"application/vnd.jupyter.widget-view+json":{"model_id":"07fbdc04de3d41e183034a79ef0942f0","version_major":2,"version_minor":0},"text/plain":["README.md:   0%|          | 0.00/10.5k [00:00<?, ?B/s]"]},"metadata":{},"output_type":"display_data"},{"data":{"application/vnd.jupyter.widget-view+json":{"model_id":"895b7b5c75d6496cbea7a8a9faae9f01","version_major":2,"version_minor":0},"text/plain":["sentence_bert_config.json:   0%|          | 0.00/53.0 [00:00<?, ?B/s]"]},"metadata":{},"output_type":"display_data"},{"data":{"application/vnd.jupyter.widget-view+json":{"model_id":"c63b23f8b41f4755a4bdf5fe7b818887","version_major":2,"version_minor":0},"text/plain":["config.j